{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was a prominent figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His father Raymond II was the Count of Tripoli and his mother Hodierna was the daughter of King Baldwin II of Jerusalem. Raymond III became the Count of Tripoli after the death of his father in 1152. He was only around 12 years old at the time and his mother acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III played an important role in the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century. He was a close ally of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and participated in several military campaigns against the Fatimid Caliphate. In 1164 Raymond III was captured by the Fatimids during the Battle of Harim. He was imprisoned for several years before being released in 1168.\n\n*Conflict with Saladin*\nIn the 1170s Raymond III became increasingly concerned about the rise of Saladin who had united the Fatimid and Ayyubid dynasties. Saladin began to attack the Kingdom of Jerusalem and Raymond III played a key role in defending the kingdom. In 1174 Raymond III led a delegation to Constantinople to seek the help of Emperor Manuel I Komnenos. The emperor agreed to provide military aid to the Kingdom of Jerusalem but the help never arrived.\n\nRaymond III continued to play an important role in the Kingdom of Jerusalem until his death in 1187. He died during the Siege of Acre which was led by Saladin. Raymond III was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV of Antioch who became the Count of Tripoli.\n\n*Legacy*\nRaymond III is remembered as a prominent figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century. He played an important role in defending the kingdom against the Fatimid and Ayyubid dynasties. Despite his efforts the kingdom eventually fell to Saladin in 1187. Raymond III's legacy is still studied by historians today who are interested in the history of the Crusades and the Kingdom of Jerusalem.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004134069196879864}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.318112355074845e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -1.245502233505249}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.07054182887077332}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.015811331570148468}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -3.576279254957626e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.00033574970439076424}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0010117044439539313}, {"token": "115", "logprob": -1.2517053619376384e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -8.356920443475246e-05}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.15505382418632507}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.008026479743421078}, {"token": "118", "logprob": -0.0001134936828748323}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -7.152813486754894e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.24219582974910736}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.002609077375382185}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.004225825425237417}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.11819026619195938}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.022028865292668343}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.0967314665322192e-05}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -0.0010394490091130137}, {"token": " II", "logprob": -4.0531240301788785e-06}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.9670190811157227}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -3.910141094820574e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.00017328571993857622}, {"token": " H", "logprob": -0.016163760796189308}, {"token": "odi", "logprob": -3.57628505298635e-06}, {"token": "erna", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.0041192076168954e-05}, {"token": " Jerusalem", "logprob": -0.00042119549470953643}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.0422452837228775}, {"token": " Raymond